# Arkham House project conventions

Personal Wikipedia bibliography gallery. Keep changes small, match existing patterns, and prefer plain Node/HTML/CSS over new libraries or bundlers.

## README (required maintenance)

The canonical project overview and **npm script reference** is [`README.md`](../../README.md) at the repo root.

**Whenever you change** any of the following, you MUST update `README.md` in the same work (same PR / same task) so it stays accurate:

- `package.json` scripts or CLI flags in `scripts/cli.js`
- New or removed crawl/sync tasks under `scripts/tasks/`
- Data model fields in `books.json` / `edits.json` or edit-dialog / API behavior
- Build or deploy workflow (`build.js`, `server.js`, `build/` output)
- Major viewer features (filters, overlays, external links, read-only vs serve mode)
- Project layout (new top-level dirs or moved entry points)

Do not edit the plan files in `.cursor/plans/` for this — only `README.md`. Keep the script tables and “How it works” section in sync; a one-line mention in the README is not enough if a whole command or workflow changed.

## Layout

| Path | Role |
|------|------|
| `viewer.html` | Source UI markup (loads `js/viewer-bundle.js`). Not the deploy artifact. |
| `js/viewer/` | Viewer app source modules; `npm run bundle-viewer` → `js/viewer-bundle.js` |
| `css/` | All viewer styles — **no inline `<style>` in viewer.html** |
| `scripts/` | Wikipedia crawler and data-sync CLI (CommonJS modules) |
| `data/books.json`, `data/books.js`, `data/descriptions.js` | Scraped catalog: full JSON on disk; viewer loads slim `BOOKS` + `BOOK_DESCRIPTIONS` |
| `data/edits.json`, `data/edits.js` | Manual edits keyed by book id (`window.BOOK_EDITS`); written only by `server.js` — only fields differing from scraped `books.json` (plus `hidden`, `deleted`); `npm run compact-edits` prunes redundant rows |
| `my_collection/` | Sample owned-books CSV + `collection.js`; shipped in all builds; gear settings toggle sample (`arkham-sample-collection`) vs own (`arkham-collection`) |
| `covers/` | Downloaded cover images |
| `examples/` | Saved Wikipedia HTML for `--local` crawls |
| `build.js` → `build/` | Static site (`npm run build`): read-only viewer, `css/viewer.css`, slim books + descriptions JS, no `books.json` in deploy |
| `server.js` | Dev server with PATCH/upload (`npm run serve`, port 8742) |
| `test/` | Node tests (`npm test`); `test/fixtures/` for small JSON fixtures |
| `scripts/lib/viewer-*.js` | Testable viewer domain logic; synced into `js/viewer/00-viewer-*.js` on bundle |

## CSS

Styles live in `css/`, linked from `viewer.html` in this order (load order matters):

1. `variables.css` — `:root` custom properties
2. `base.css` — reset, body, filter-mode themes
3. `header.css` — header, search, sort, stats
4. `attribution.css`, `settings.css`, `order-dialog.css` — footer, gear, reorder dialog
5. `cards.css` — grid, cards, badges, card content
6. `tags.css` — shared tag chip styles (detail + edit dialog)
7. `edit-dialog.css` — edit modal
8. `book-detail.css` — detail overlay + desktop `@media (min-width: 641px)`
9. `cover-lightbox.css` — full-screen cover view
10. `mobile.css` — `@media (max-width: 640px)` overrides

Canonical load order: [`scripts/css-manifest.js`](../../scripts/css-manifest.js) (`VIEWER_CSS_FILES`). Keep [`viewer.html`](../../viewer.html) link tags in sync.

When adding styles: put them in the most specific file above; add a new file only for a distinct UI surface. After CSS changes, update the manifest and `viewer.html` links.

## Crawler / scripts JavaScript

Use **CommonJS** (`require` / `module.exports`). No bundler, no TypeScript, no new frameworks.

```
scripts/
  index.js       CLI entry — init state, warnings, dispatch tasks
  config.js      Paths, IMPRINTS, Wikipedia constants
  cli.js         parseArgs, getScriptMode, scrape warnings
  state.js       Shared runtime: args, caches, request counters
  lib/           Reusable domain logic (one concern per file)
  tasks/         One file per npm command / operation mode
```

Guidelines:
- New CLI modes → add flag in `cli.js`, handler in `tasks/`, wire in `index.js`, add npm script in `package.json`, **update `README.md`**
- Shared mutable state goes in `state.js`, not new globals
- Constants and paths go in `config.js`
- Wikipedia parsing → `lib/wiki-*.js`; cover logic → `lib/covers-*.js`; scrape I/O → `lib/books.js`; user edits → `lib/edits.js`
- Scrapes use `replaceScrapedBooks`: stable `id` per bibliography line (`wikipediaUrl + listYear`); scripts never write `edits.json`
- `npm run dedupe-book-ids` splits duplicate ids; shared `hidden` edits move to reprint ids only
- Scripts may read `edits.json` read-only to skip cover download/reconcile when `coverImageFile` is set in edits, or skip description sync when `description` is set in edits
- Viewer/build merge scraped books with edits at read time (`js/book-edits.js`, `js/book-layers.js`)
- Scrape modes that overwrite data show a warning unless `--yes`

Entry point for all npm crawl/sync scripts: `node scripts/index.js`

## Viewer app JavaScript

App logic lives in `js/viewer/` (eight topic files, one shared IIFE) and ships as `js/viewer-bundle.js`. Edit the partials, then `npm run bundle-viewer` (also runs at the start of `npm run build`). Follow existing patterns: vanilla JS, `localStorage` for want-list (`arkham-want-list`), sample collection (`arkham-sample-collection`, seeded from CSV), own collection (`arkham-collection`), and active mode (`arkham-collection-source`); gear settings can reset sample to CSV defaults; `getVisibleBooks()` for filtered order; descriptions editable via the edit dialog (stored in `edits.json`).

`js/book-edits.js` merges `window.BOOKS` with `window.BOOK_EDITS` at startup; `js/book-layers.js` attaches `window.BOOK_TAGS`. Linked from `viewer.html`, copied in `build.js` via `copyDirectory("js")`.

Viewer domain logic that affects behavior (sort, filters, collection state, parsing helpers, etc.) belongs in `scripts/lib/` as pure CommonJS, tested under `test/`, then wired into the viewer via generated `js/viewer/00-*.js` partials (`scripts/sync-viewer-module.js` + `scripts/viewer-sync-config.js`, run by `npm run bundle-viewer`). Do not leave behavior-only logic untested in `js/viewer/` alone.

## Tests (required for relevant features)

**Always add or update tests** when you change behavior in any of these areas (same task / same PR — do not defer):

- `scripts/lib/` domain logic (crawler parsing, book order, edits, collection matching, viewer sort/filters, etc.)
- `server.js` API or validation rules
- Data transforms, merge rules, or export formats
- Viewer features with non-trivial state machines (filters, sort modes, collection cycles, etc.)

Skip tests only for purely visual/CSS tweaks, copy/markup-only edits, or one-line fixes with no behavior change. When in doubt, add a focused test.

**How to test**

- Runner: Node built-in `node:test` + `node:assert/strict` — no new test frameworks or devDependencies
- Command: `npm test` (runs `test/**/*.test.js`)
- Location: `test/<topic>.test.js`; shared fixtures in `test/fixtures/`
- Scripts: put testable logic in `scripts/lib/<name>.js`, export pure functions, `require()` from tests
- Viewer: shared module in `scripts/lib/viewer-*.js` → `scripts/sync-viewer-module.js` → `js/viewer/00-viewer-*.js`; viewer partials call `viewerSort` / `viewerFilters` / etc.; run `npm run bundle-viewer` after changes
- After adding tests or scripts: update `README.md` `npm test` row if workflow changed

**Test quality**

- Cover happy path and important edge cases (empty input, toggles/cycles, mutually exclusive states)
- Prefer small inline book/object fixtures over loading full `data/books.json` unless integration coverage is the point
- Keep tests readable: one behavior per test, descriptive names

## Data and build workflow

- Edit books in browser via `npm run serve` (writes `data/edits.json`, including `hidden`, `deleted`, etc.); run targeted syncs when possible
- **Delete** in the edit dialog sets `deleted: true` in edits (removed from UI everywhere; scraped row stays in `books.json`)
- **Hide** sets `hidden: true` in edits (still visible on localhost with “Show hidden”)
- Full crawls / sync scripts overwrite scraped fields in `books.json` only; edits in `edits.json` (dates, covers, hidden, etc.) survive
- Legacy `hidden` on scraped books is auto-migrated to `edits.json` when scripts write `books.json`
- After data or viewer changes for deploy: `npm run build` → `build/`
- Do not hand-edit `build/` or generated `data/books.js` / `data/edits.js` — edit source and rebuild/re-crawl
- Do not modify generated SDK files unless explicitly asked

## General editing principles

- Minimize diff scope; don't refactor unrelated code
- Match naming, formatting, and abstractions of surrounding files
- Comments only for non-obvious logic
- Add tests for relevant behavior changes (see **Tests** above); run `npm test` before finishing
